<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">State of El Salvador</h1><p class="st-meta">1824 CE – 1841 CE · capital San Salvador</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Indigo-producing member state of the Central American Federation, the liberal heartland that backed Francisco Morazán to the end.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Salvadoran liberals led by the priest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-matias-delgado">José Matías Delgado</a> had resisted annexation to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-mexican-empire">First Mexican Empire</a>, even offering in 1822 to join the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>. With the creation of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/federal-republic-of-central-america">Federal Republic of Central America</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">El Salvador</a> became one of its five states under its own constitution of 1824, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-manuel-rodriguez">Juan Manuel Rodríguez</a> as first head of state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The economy depended on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indigo">indigo</a> grown on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hacienda">haciendas</a> and exported through Guatemalan merchants, a source of rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala-city">Guatemala City</a>. Salvadoran liberals fought Guatemalan conservatives in the federal civil war of 1826 to 1829. In 1833 the Nonualco people rose under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anastasio-aquino">Anastasio Aquino</a> against land seizures and forced recruitment. From 1834 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a> hosted the federal government, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-morazan">Francisco Morazán</a> himself served as head of state in 1839 and 1840.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-morazan">Morazán</a>'s defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rafael-carrera">Rafael Carrera</a> in 1840, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-el-salvador">El Salvador</a> lost its federal role, and a constitution of February 1841 declared it a sovereign state, the Republic of El <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salvador">Salvador</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="republic, 1824 CE – 1841 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 38 L50 20 L80 38 Z"/><path d="M24 42 H76 M24 80 H76"/><path d="M30 44 V78 M43 44 V78 M57 44 V78 M70 44 V78"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">republic</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:96.00%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1824 CE – 1841 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>San Salvador</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Juan Manuel Rodríguez, Francisco Morazán</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/el-salvador">El Salvador</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Estado de El Salvador</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1832&amp;focus=state-of-el-salvador" aria-label="State of El Salvador on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2236 1668 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of El Salvador lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2556 1924L2525 1889M2556 1924L2556 1924"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2525 1889h0M2556 1924h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2556 1924h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2556 1924h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks San Salvador. <a href="/atlas?year=1832&amp;focus=state-of-el-salvador">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
